  "lessons": [
    {
      "domain_id": "vscode",
      "title": "VS Code \u2014 Core concepts",
      "concepts": [
        "Command Palette: the Command Palette (Ctrl+Shift+P) is VS Code's universal command launcher: type a few letters of any command \u2014 menu items, extension actions, settings \u2014 and run it without touching the mouse.",
        "Keyboard-first navigation compounds: the palette removes the need to memorize dozens of individual shortcuts.",
        "Launch.json in .vscode/ defines debug configurations: type (debugger, e.g. node/python), request (launch vs attach), program (what to run), plus args, env, and preLaunchTask.",
        "Multi-cursor: for cross-file renames, F2 (rename symbol) is the language-aware tool \u2014 multi-cursor is for the local, mechanical case."
      ],
      "example": {
        "lang": "json",
        "code": "{\n  \"editor.formatOnSave\": true,\n  \"editor.defaultFormatter\": \"esbenp.prettier-vscode\"\n}",
        "walkthrough": "VS Code settings layer: user settings (personal defaults) < workspace settings (.vscode/settings.json, committed). Committing the workspace file makes format-on-save, the formatter choice, and rulers consistent for every clone \u2014 the repo carries its own editor contract. Personal preferences stay in user settings; project requirements go in the workspace file."
      },
      "pitfalls": [
        "Common trap: the team must use the same OS for settings to apply.",
        "Common trap: multi-cursor doesn't exist in VS Code.",
        "Common trap: emmet only works in .html files.",
        "Common trap: edit keybindings.json to add every command manually."
      ],
      "try_it": "Try it now in the Terminal tab \u2192"
    },
    {
      "domain_id": "intellij-pycharm",
      "title": "IntelliJ / PyCharm \u2014 Core concepts",
      "concepts": [
        "Refactoring: shift+F6 Rename is a language-aware refactoring: it updates declarations, references, and (optionally) related files while respecting scope \u2014 a comment mentioning the old name isn't touched, a shadowing local isn't renamed.",
        "Rename: the IDE's refactoring engine exists precisely so renames are safe, previewable operations, not hope-driven edits.",
        "Navigation: professional IDE fluency is largely navigation fluency.",
        "Search Everywhere (double-Shift) finds classes, files, symbols, and IDE actions by name; Find in Path (Ctrl+Shift+F) searches file contents with regex and scope filters; Alt+F7 finds usages of the symbol at the caret.",
        "Debugger: the debugger pauses at breakpoints and lets you inspect the full state: Evaluate Expression (Alt+F8) runs arbitrary expressions in the current frame, Watches track values across steps, and conditional breakpoints stop only on the failing case."
      ],
      "example": {
        "lang": "text",
        "code": "// rename class `OrderProcessor` (used in 40 files) to `OrderService`",
        "walkthrough": "Shift+F6 Rename is a language-aware refactoring: it updates declarations, references, and (optionally) related files while respecting scope \u2014 a comment mentioning the old name isn't touched, a shadowing local isn't renamed. Find-and-replace is textual and blind."
      },
      "pitfalls": [
        "Common trap: select the block and use Extract Method (Ctrl+Alt+M).",
        "Common trap: run configurations can't take arguments.",
        "Common trap: press Shift twice (Search Everywhere) or Ctrl+Shift+F (Find in Path).",
        "Common trap: the IDE always runs the currently open file."
      ],
      "try_it": "Work through this in the Terminal tab \u2192"
    },
    {
      "domain_id": "visual-studio",
      "title": "Visual Studio \u2014 Core concepts",
      "concepts": [
        "Debugging: F5 = Start Debugging (debugger attached: breakpoints, variable inspection, slower startup); Ctrl+F5 = Start Without Debugging (just runs).",
        "F5 vs Ctrl+F5: if you're not debugging, Ctrl+F5 skips the debugger overhead.",
        "Attach to Process connects the debugger to an already-running process \u2014 breakpoints hit in the live service, with call stacks and variable inspection.",
        "Immediate Window: the Immediate Window evaluates expressions and statements in the paused program's context \u2014 call methods, inspect objects, even assign variables to steer the session.",
        "Debug/Release are build contracts: Debug disables optimizations and emits full symbols for diagnosability; Release enables optimizations for performance."
      ],
      "example": {
        "lang": "text",
        "code": "# Solution Configurations dropdown: Debug | Release",
        "walkthrough": "Debug/Release are build contracts: Debug disables optimizations and emits full symbols for diagnosability; Release enables optimizations for performance. Shipping Debug to production-adjacent environments is slower and can behave differently (timing, inlining). The dropdown also drives conditional compilation (#if DEBUG) \u2014 diagnostics code that must never ship."
      },
      "pitfalls": [
        "Common trap: open the Immediate Window (Debug > Windows > Immediate) during a debug session and evaluate expressions / call methods in the current context.",
        "Common trap: the Immediate Window only runs in Release builds.",
        "Common trap: build configurations (Debug/Release) control symbols, optimizations, and conditional compilation.",
        "Common trap: attach to Process only works for web apps."
      ],
      "try_it": "Work through this in the Terminal tab \u2192"
    },
    {
      "domain_id": "debugging-workflows",
      "title": "Debugging Workflows \u2014 Core concepts",
      "concepts": [
        "Reproduction: a reliable reproduction is the foundation: it turns 'sometimes' into a deterministic trigger, gives you a before/after oracle for the fix, and often reveals the cause while you minimize it.",
        "Conditional breakpoints (break when order.Id == 7431, or when total < 0) stop only on the failing case; logpoints ('logpoints'/'tracepoints') print values without stopping at all.",
        "Bisection is binary search on history: git bisect checks the midpoint commit, marks good/bad, and halves the range \u2014 ~9 steps for 300 commits instead of 300."
      ],
      "example": {
        "lang": "text",
        "code": "System.NullReferenceException: Object reference not set...\n   at Shop.Orders.ApplyDiscount(Order o) in Orders.cs:line 42\n   at Shop.Cart.Checkout() in Cart.cs:line 17",
        "walkthrough": "Read traces top-down: exception type + message (what), then frames (where) \u2014 the first frame in your code is the prime suspect (Orders.cs:42 dereferences something null). Framework frames below/above are context. The trace is the program telling you exactly where it gave up; ignoring it to read from main() discards the best evidence you have."
      },
      "pitfalls": [
        "Common trap: bisect: check the behavior at the midpoint of the suspect range (git bisect for regressions, or halve the input), discard the innocent half, repeat.",
        "Common trap: explain it to a rubber duck (or a teammate, or write it up).",
        "Common trap: set a breakpoint at the loop start and press F5 10,000 times.",
        "Common trap: understanding everything precedes any fix."
      ],
      "try_it": "Work through this in the Terminal tab \u2192"
    },
    {
      "domain_id": "extensions-settings",
      "title": "Extensions & Settings \u2014 Core concepts",
      "concepts": [
        "EditorConfig: manual reconfiguration is slow and lossy; the project-level contract (.vscode/, .editorconfig) covers team needs, sync covers personal ones.",
        "Team setup: extensions.json recommendations prompt every fresh clone to install the team's extension set in one click \u2014 the project's tooling becomes self-describing. Treat your setup as code.",
        "Principle: it's the lowest common denominator that actually works."
      ],
      "example": {
        "lang": "json",
        "code": "{\n  \"recommendations\": [\"esbenp.prettier-vscode\", \"dbaeumer.vscode-eslint\"]\n}",
        "walkthrough": "extensions.json recommendations prompt every fresh clone to install the team's extension set in one click \u2014 the project's tooling becomes self-describing. Word-of-mouth onboarding is lossy; forced installs are heavy-handed. Recommendations hit the sweet spot: visible, one-click, and versioned with the code they serve."
      },
      "pitfalls": [
        "Common trap: review what each extension sends (many offer opt-outs), keep useful crash/usage telemetry for tools you trust, and disable or remove extensions that over-collect.",
        "Common trap: everyone discovers extensions by word of mouth.",
        "Common trap: force-install extensions via group policy.",
        "Common trap: formatting can't be shared across different editors."
      ],
      "try_it": "Try it now in the Terminal tab \u2192"
    },
    {
      "domain_id": "terminal-tasks",
      "title": "Terminal & Tasks \u2014 Core concepts",
      "concepts": [
        "Integrated terminal: the integrated terminal (Ctrl+`) runs in the project directory with the editor's environment, and \u2014 critically \u2014 file:line references in output become clickable links that jump to the exact location.",
        "Tasks.json (or npm scripts, Make targets, justfile) captures the project's command vocabulary as named, runnable, versioned entries \u2014 bound to shortcuts, composable (test depends on build), and identical for everyone.",
        "Npx resolves and runs the project's local node_modules/.bin binaries \u2014 the version the project pinned, not whatever's global."
      ],
      "example": {
        "lang": "json",
        "code": "{\n  \"version\": \"2.0.0\",\n  \"tasks\": [\n    { \"label\": \"build\", \"type\": \"shell\", \"command\": \"npm run build\" },\n    { \"label\": \"test\", \"type\": \"shell\", \"command\": \"npm test\" }\n  ]\n}",
        "walkthrough": "tasks.json (or npm scripts, Make targets, justfile) captures the project's command vocabulary as named, runnable, versioned entries \u2014 bound to shortcuts, composable (test depends on build), and identical for everyone. Retyped-from-memory commands drift; README commands rot. The runnable definition is the documentation."
      },
      "pitfalls": [
        "Common trap: use npx (or the package manager's dlx) to run one-off binaries without installing.",
        "Common trap: define tasks in .vscode/tasks.json (build, test, lint) with keyboard shortcuts.",
        "Common trap: alt+Tab to a separate terminal app.",
        "Common trap: npx only works for npm's own commands."
      ],
      "try_it": "Try it now in the Terminal tab \u2192"
    },
    {
      "domain_id": "vscode",
      "title": "Developer Tooling - Core concepts",
      "concepts": [
        "Editors (VS Code) are fast and extended via extensions; IDEs (JetBrains) bundle deep language understanding - indexing, safe refactoring, call hierarchies. Match the tool to the codebase size and language, not to habit.",
        "Debuggers beat print statements for state you cannot see: breakpoints (including conditional ones), step over/into/out, watch expressions, and the call stack. Reproduce the bug first, then bisect with breakpoints.",
        "Linters (ESLint, Pylint) catch bug patterns; formatters (Prettier, Black) end style debates. Run both in CI so the main branch is always clean and reviews discuss logic, not whitespace.",
        "Git CLI is universal and scriptable - learn it first. GUI clients (GitHub Desktop, lazygit) shine at staging individual hunks and reviewing diffs; the concepts transfer because it is the same DAG underneath.",
        "Dev containers and remote development (SSH, WSL) put the toolchain in version control. 'Works on my machine' dies when the machine is defined in a Dockerfile.",
        "launch.json and tasks.json make F5 debugging and builds repeatable per project. Commit the portable parts; keep personal absolute paths out of shared config."
      ],
      "example": {
        "lang": "shell",
        "code": "code --install-extension esbenp.prettier-vscode\ncat > .vscode/settings.json <<'EOF'\n{\n  \"editor.formatOnSave\": true,\n  \"editor.defaultFormatter\": \"esbenp.prettier-vscode\"\n}\nEOF",
        "walkthrough": "Install the formatter from the terminal so setup is scriptable, then pin format-on-save in workspace settings. Every contributor gets identical formatting without thinking about it - style becomes infrastructure, not discussion."
      },
      "pitfalls": [
        "Common trap: debugging only with print statements - learn conditional breakpoints and watch expressions for intermittent bugs.",
        "Common trap: committing .vscode/ with absolute personal paths - keep workspace settings portable or gitignore them.",
        "Common trap: disabling a linter repo-wide to silence one noisy rule - use a per-line disable with an explanatory comment instead.",
        "Common trap: resolving merge conflicts by picking one side for every hunk - read each hunk; conflicts often need a combination, then tests."
      ],
      "try_it": "Work through this in the Labs tab.",
      "domain_id_remapped_from": "ides-tooling",
      "merged_from": "cbt-individual/ides-tooling"
    },
    {
      "title": "From Static Diagrams to Living Systems",
      "domain_id": "systems-anatomy",
      "concepts": [
        "A textbook diagram shows you one part in isolation \u2014 a living systems view shows you how all parts of Ides Tooling move together in sequence",
        "The four-step systems anatomy method: (1) See the whole in context, (2) Cut it open to reveal layers, (3) Walk through the process step-by-step, (4) Pull apart into labeled pieces",
        "Apply this to any system in this course: start with the trigger action, trace through every component it touches, watch the data flow",
        "Interview/exam power: when asked 'how does X work?', narrate the journey \u2014 'it starts here, then...'. This demonstrates senior-level systems thinking"
      ],
      "example": {
        "lang": "text",
        "code": "TRIGGER event occurs\n  \u2192 First system receives it (see the handoff)\n  \u2192 Processing happens in sequence (watch each step)\n  \u2192 State changes propagate (follow the ripple)\n  \u2192 Final outcome delivered\n\nEach arrow is a living handoff, not a static box.",
        "walkthrough": "This is the 'jet engine cutaway' for your field: instead of memorizing isolated facts, you see the entire flow as a moving sequence. Practice narrating this journey out loud."
      },
      "pitfalls": [
        "Memorizing terms without understanding the processes they participate in",
        "Drawing architecture diagrams as boxes without showing the data flow between them",
        "Answering 'how does X work?' with a definition instead of walking through the sequence"
      ],
      "try_it": "Pick any process in this course. Write out the full journey from trigger to outcome, naming every system touched."
    },
    {
      "title": "Anatomy of VS Code",
      "domain_id": "systems-anatomy",
      "concepts": [
        "VS Code is a multi-step process within VS Code: trace the full sequence from initiation to completion",
        "See it as a journey: identify the trigger, follow each handoff, watch state change at every step",
        "Every step depends on the previous \u2014 a failure at any point breaks the chain. Map these dependencies",
        "Connect VS Code to the broader VS Code ecosystem \u2014 nothing operates in isolation"
      ],
      "example": {
        "lang": "text",
        "code": "Step 1: Initiation\n  VS Code begins \u2014 what triggers it?\n\nStep 2: Processing\n  Data flows through \u2014 what transforms it?\n\nStep 3: Handoff\n  Result passes to next system \u2014 what is the interface?\n\nStep 4: Completion\n  Final state reached \u2014 how do you verify?",
        "walkthrough": "Apply the four-step anatomy method to VS Code. Draw it as a flowing sequence, not a static diagram."
      },
      "pitfalls": [
        "Treating VS Code as an isolated component instead of part of a living flow",
        "Skipping the dependency mapping \u2014 not asking 'what does this step need from the previous?'",
        "Memorizing the steps without understanding WHY each one exists"
      ],
      "try_it": "Whiteboard the full VS Code flow from memory. Label every handoff and dependency."
    },
