  "resources": {
    "cheatsheet": {
      "title": "IDEs & Developer Tooling \u2014 Cheat Sheet",
      "sections": [
        {
          "heading": "VS Code",
          "items": [
            "Command Palette \u2014 the Command Palette (Ctrl+Shift+P) is VS Code's universal command launcher: type a few letters of any command \u2014 menu items, extension actions, settings \u2014 and run it without touching the mouse.",
            "Keyboard-first navigation compounds \u2014 the palette removes the need to memorize dozens of individual shortcuts.",
            "Launch.json in .vscode/ defines debug configurations \u2014 type (debugger, e.g. node/python), request (launch vs attach), program (what to run), plus args, env, and preLaunchTask.",
            "Multi-cursor \u2014 for cross-file renames, F2 (rename symbol) is the language-aware tool \u2014 multi-cursor is for the local, mechanical case.",
            "Common trap \u2014 the team must use the same OS for settings to apply.",
            "Common trap \u2014 multi-cursor doesn't exist in VS Code.",
            "Common trap \u2014 emmet only works in .html files.",
            "Common trap \u2014 edit keybindings.json to add every command manually.",
            "VS Code settings layer \u2014 user settings (personal defaults) < workspace settings (.vscode/settings.json, committed). Committing the workspace file makes format-on-save, the formatter choice, and rulers consistent for every clone \u2014 the repo carries its own editor contract. Personal p\u2026"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "IntelliJ / PyCharm",
          "items": [
            "Refactoring \u2014 shift+F6 Rename is a language-aware refactoring: it updates declarations, references, and (optionally) related files while respecting scope \u2014 a comment mentioning the old name isn't touched, a shadowing local isn't renamed.",
            "Rename \u2014 the IDE's refactoring engine exists precisely so renames are safe, previewable operations, not hope-driven edits.",
            "Navigation \u2014 professional IDE fluency is largely navigation fluency.",
            "Search Everywhere \u2014 Search Everywhere (double-Shift) finds classes, files, symbols, and IDE actions by name; Find in Path (Ctrl+Shift+F) searches file contents with regex and scope filters; Alt+F7 finds usages of the symbol at the caret.",
            "Debugger \u2014 the debugger pauses at breakpoints and lets you inspect the full state: Evaluate Expression (Alt+F8) runs arbitrary expressions in the current frame, Watches track values across steps, and conditional breakpoints stop only on the failing case.",
            "Common trap \u2014 select the block and use Extract Method (Ctrl+Alt+M).",
            "Common trap \u2014 run configurations can't take arguments.",
            "Common trap \u2014 press Shift twice (Search Everywhere) or Ctrl+Shift+F (Find in Path).",
            "Common trap \u2014 the IDE always runs the currently open file.",
            "Shift+F6 Rename is a language-aware refactoring \u2014 it updates declarations, references, and (optionally) related files while respecting scope \u2014 a comment mentioning the old name isn't touched, a shadowing local isn't renamed. Find-and-replace is textual and blind."
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Visual Studio",
          "items": [
            "Debugging \u2014 F5 = Start Debugging (debugger attached: breakpoints, variable inspection, slower startup); Ctrl+F5 = Start Without Debugging (just runs).",
            "F5 vs Ctrl+F5 \u2014 if you're not debugging, Ctrl+F5 skips the debugger overhead.",
            "Attach to Process connects the debugger to an already-running process \u2014 breakpoints hit in the live service, with call stacks and variable inspection.",
            "Immediate Window \u2014 the Immediate Window evaluates expressions and statements in the paused program's context \u2014 call methods, inspect objects, even assign variables to steer the session.",
            "Debug/Release are build contracts \u2014 Debug disables optimizations and emits full symbols for diagnosability; Release enables optimizations for performance.",
            "Common trap \u2014 open the Immediate Window (Debug > Windows > Immediate) during a debug session and evaluate expressions / call methods in the current context.",
            "Common trap \u2014 the Immediate Window only runs in Release builds.",
            "Common trap \u2014 build configurations (Debug/Release) control symbols, optimizations, and conditional compilation.",
            "Common trap \u2014 attach to Process only works for web apps.",
            "Debug/Release are build contracts \u2014 Debug disables optimizations and emits full symbols for diagnosability; Release enables optimizations for performance. Shipping Debug to production-adjacent environments is slower and can behave differently (timing, inlining). The dropdown also drives condi\u2026"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Debugging Workflows",
          "items": [
            "Reproduction \u2014 a reliable reproduction is the foundation: it turns 'sometimes' into a deterministic trigger, gives you a before/after oracle for the fix, and often reveals the cause while you minimize it.",
            "Conditional \u2014 Conditional breakpoints (break when order.Id == 7431, or when total < 0) stop only on the failing case; logpoints ('logpoints'/'tracepoints') print values without stopping at all.",
            "Bisection is binary search on history \u2014 git bisect checks the midpoint commit, marks good/bad, and halves the range \u2014 ~9 steps for 300 commits instead of 300.",
            "Common trap \u2014 bisect: check the behavior at the midpoint of the suspect range (git bisect for regressions, or halve the input), discard the innocent half, repeat.",
            "Common trap \u2014 explain it to a rubber duck (or a teammate, or write it up).",
            "Common trap \u2014 set a breakpoint at the loop start and press F5 10,000 times.",
            "Common trap \u2014 understanding everything precedes any fix.",
            "Read traces top-down \u2014 exception type + message (what), then frames (where) \u2014 the first frame in your code is the prime suspect (Orders.cs:42 dereferences something null). Framework frames below/above are context. The trace is the program telling you exactly where it gave up; ign\u2026"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Extensions & Settings",
          "items": [
            "EditorConfig \u2014 manual reconfiguration is slow and lossy; the project-level contract (.vscode/, .editorconfig) covers team needs, sync covers personal ones.",
            "Team setup \u2014 extensions.json recommendations prompt every fresh clone to install the team's extension set in one click \u2014 the project's tooling becomes self-describing. Treat your setup as code.",
            "Principle \u2014 it's the lowest common denominator that actually works.",
            "Common trap \u2014 review what each extension sends (many offer opt-outs), keep useful crash/usage telemetry for tools you trust, and disable or remove extensions that over-collect.",
            "Common trap \u2014 everyone discovers extensions by word of mouth.",
            "Common trap \u2014 force-install extensions via group policy.",
            "Common trap \u2014 formatting can't be shared across different editors."
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Terminal & Tasks",
          "items": [
            "Integrated terminal \u2014 the integrated terminal (Ctrl+`) runs in the project directory with the editor's environment, and \u2014 critically \u2014 file:line references in output become clickable links that jump to the exact location.",
            "Tasks.json \u2014 Tasks.json (or npm scripts, Make targets, justfile) captures the project's command vocabulary as named, runnable, versioned entries \u2014 bound to shortcuts, composable (test depends on build), and identical for everyone.",
            "Npx resolves and runs the project's local node_modules/.bin binaries \u2014 the version the project pinned, not whatever's global.",
            "Common trap \u2014 use npx (or the package manager's dlx) to run one-off binaries without installing.",
            "Common trap \u2014 define tasks in .vscode/tasks.json (build, test, lint) with keyboard shortcuts.",
            "Common trap \u2014 alt+Tab to a separate terminal app.",
            "Common trap \u2014 npx only works for npm's own commands."
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    "glossary": [
      {
        "term": "IDE",
        "def": "Rename: the IDE's refactoring engine exists precisely so renames are safe, previewable operations, not hope-driven edits."
      },
      {
        "term": "VS",
        "def": "launch.json in .vscode/ defines debug configurations: type (debugger, e.g. node/python), request (launch vs attach), program (what to run), plus args, env, and preLaunchTask."
      },
      {
        "term": "VS Code",
        "def": "Emmet is built into VS Code: CSS-like abbreviations (ul>li.item*5, div.container>header+main+footer) expand with Tab into full markup, including numbering ($), attributes, and lorem text. It works in HTML, JSX, and more."
      },
      {
        "term": "Debug",
        "def": "Debug/Release are build contracts: Debug disables optimizations and emits full symbols for diagnosability; Release enables optimizations for performance."
      },
      {
        "term": "Immediate Window",
        "def": "Immediate Window: the Immediate Window evaluates expressions and statements in the paused program's context \u2014 call methods, inspect objects, even assign variables to steer the session."
      },
      {
        "term": "SDK",
        "def": "NuGet package references (<PackageReference> in the SDK-style csproj) are versioned, restorable (dotnet restore fetches them on any machine), and transitive \u2014 the package's own dependencies resolve automatically. Committed DLLs rot (whic\u2026"
      },
      {
        "term": "git",
        "def": "Bisection is binary search on history: git bisect checks the midpoint commit, marks good/bad, and halves the range \u2014 ~9 steps for 300 commits instead of 300."
      },
      {
        "term": "npm",
        "def": "Tasks.json (or npm scripts, Make targets, justfile) captures the project's command vocabulary as named, runnable, versioned entries \u2014 bound to shortcuts, composable (test depends on build), and identical for everyone."
      },
      {
        "term": "Ctrl+F5",
        "def": "F5 vs Ctrl+F5: if you're not debugging, Ctrl+F5 skips the debugger overhead."
      },
      {
        "term": "Release",
        "def": "Debug/Release are build contracts: Debug disables optimizations and emits full symbols for diagnosability; Release enables optimizations for performance."
      },
      {
        "term": "Debug/Release",
        "def": "Debug/Release are build contracts: Debug disables optimizations and emits full symbols for diagnosability; Release enables optimizations for performance."
      },
      {
        "term": "Find-and-replace",
        "def": "Shift+F6 Rename is a language-aware refactoring: it updates declarations, references, and (optionally) related files while respecting scope \u2014 a comment mentioning the old name isn't touched, a shadowing local isn't renamed. Find-and-repl\u2026"
      },
      {
        "term": "Search Everywhere",
        "def": "Search Everywhere (double-Shift) finds classes, files, symbols, and IDE actions by name; Find in Path (Ctrl+Shift+F) searches file contents with regex and scope filters; Alt+F7 finds usages of the symbol at the caret."
      },
      {
        "term": "EditorConfig",
        "def": "EditorConfig: manual reconfiguration is slow and lossy; the project-level contract (.vscode/, .editorconfig) covers team needs, sync covers personal ones."
      },
      {
        "term": "Attach",
        "def": "Attach to Process connects the debugger to an already-running process \u2014 breakpoints hit in the live service, with call stacks and variable inspection. Requirements: matching symbols (PDBs) and appropriate permissions."
      },
      {
        "term": "Debugging",
        "def": "Debugging: F5 = Start Debugging (debugger attached: breakpoints, variable inspection, slower startup); Ctrl+F5 = Start Without Debugging (just runs)."
      },
      {
        "term": "Bisection",
        "def": "Bisection is binary search on history: git bisect checks the midpoint commit, marks good/bad, and halves the range \u2014 ~9 steps for 300 commits instead of 300."
      },
      {
        "term": "Ctrl+Shift+P",
        "def": "Command Palette: the Command Palette (Ctrl+Shift+P) is VS Code's universal command launcher: type a few letters of any command \u2014 menu items, extension actions, settings \u2014 and run it without touching the mouse."
      },
      {
        "term": "Shift+F6 Rename",
        "def": "Shift+F6 Rename is a language-aware refactoring: it updates declarations, references, and (optionally) related files while respecting scope \u2014 a comment mentioning the old name isn't touched, a shadowing local isn't renamed. Find-and-repl\u2026"
      },
      {
        "term": "UI",
        "def": "Most language extensions are front-ends for a language server, SDK, or runtime that must exist separately \u2014 the Python extension needs a Python interpreter selected, C# needs the .NET SDK, Java needs a JDK. The extension contributes UI;\u2026"
      },
      {
        "term": "JSX",
        "def": "Emmet is built into VS Code: CSS-like abbreviations (ul>li.item*5, div.container>header+main+footer) expand with Tab into full markup, including numbering ($), attributes, and lorem text. It works in HTML, JSX, and more."
      },
      {
        "term": "REPL",
        "def": "The Immediate Window evaluates expressions and statements in the paused program's context \u2014 call methods, inspect objects, even assign variables to steer the session. It's the debugger's REPL: test hypotheses (does ApplyDiscount fix the\u2026"
      },
      {
        "term": "README",
        "def": "tasks.json (or npm scripts, Make targets, justfile) captures the project's command vocabulary as named, runnable, versioned entries \u2014 bound to shortcuts, composable (test depends on build), and identical for everyone. Retyped-from-memory\u2026"
      },
      {
        "term": "Manual",
        "def": "Settings Sync (built into VS Code, backed by your account) or a dotfiles repo versions your personal configuration \u2014 extensions, settings, keybindings, snippets \u2014 making any machine yours in minutes. Manual reconfiguration is slow and lo\u2026"
      },
      {
        "term": "SDK-style",
        "def": "NuGet package references (<PackageReference> in the SDK-style csproj) are versioned, restorable (dotnet restore fetches them on any machine), and transitive \u2014 the package's own dependencies resolve automatically. Committed DLLs rot (whic\u2026"
